      "content": "To truly understand mitosis, we must learn the four distinct stages: Prophase begins when the chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes, making the process easier to follow. Next, in Metaphase, the chromosomes line up perfectly along the cell's equator, which is a helpful alignment for equal distribution. Anaphase is when the sister chromatids are pulled apart to opposite poles of the cell, a critical step to understand. Finally, Telophase reverses the process, forming two new nuclei, allowing the cells to fully understand their new, identical state.",
